  "run": {
    "final_text": "I wrote `latest.py`. When I ran it, it printed only `2025-10-12`, which is Priya's signup date.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\n\nfrom acme_client import Client\n\nusers = Client().list_users()\nlatest = max(user[\"created_at\"] for user in users)\nprint(datetime.fromtimestamp(latest, tz=timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\"))\n```\n\n- **Timestamps:** `created_at` holds Unix timestamps in seconds. The script converts them in UTC, so the result doesn't depend on the machine's local time zone.\n- **Who counts:** it looks at every user, including the ones marked `active: False`, because a signup date doesn't depend on whether the account is still active. If you only want active users, filter on `user[\"active\"]` first.\n- **Empty list:** if `list_users()` returns no users, `max()` raises an error instead of printing a date. That can't happen with the current stand-in data, but it could with a real API.",
